{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/the-crossroads-continent/1535241-side-tangent-rolling-character-stats-in-the-crossroad-continent","id":1535241,"title":"Side Tangent: Rolling Character Stats in the Crossroad Continent","path":["Home","Onyx Path","Onyx Path Worlds","The Crossroads Continent"],"posts":[{"date":"2024-05-07T19:29:15-07:00","id":1535241,"title":"Side Tangent: Rolling Character Stats in the Crossroad Continent","content":"Hello folks-<br /> <br /> It was recently a stinky thing on some social media about how people are generating their character stats in D&amp;D games. A lot of one-true-way isms, when the reality is that however your table does it is how your table does it. If the method ruins player fun, it'll get changed or the players will start to fall away. And when I talk about method, we're loosely looking at a) the most basic first edition method of rolling three 6-siders, and recording the scores in sequence. b) then is that method, but you rearrange the rolled numbers among the stats (Str, Dex, etc). These were the first ways of doing it that I used when I first learned D&amp;D during the lil pamphlet era, and when I started DMing I immediately let playing reorder the numbers so that they could play the sorts of characters they wanted. Up until adopting method (b), almost nobody played the &quot;extra classes&quot; because it was rare to have the required minimums hit just right. With method (b), you could create a character of a specific class, or manage to hit the minimums of the new classes like Druid or Ranger more often. Paladins were still super-tough to get the stats for, and don't even talk about Bards!<br /> <br /> c) I'd say is a method I think came out of Unearthed Arcana or Dragon Magazine of rolling four 6-siders and taking the highest three, and d) would be the four die method but rearranging the stats. I quickly adopted (c), and that led even more quickly to (d) for my players in the CRC, because of several factors: the first being those pesky minimums still being hard to get without rearranging, the sheer number and variety of new classes popping up in other games or in Dragon Magazine that players wanted to try, and the way that abilities and powers were starting to key off of stats. While the CRC is at heart a more down to earth setting with major magic being pretty rare, my default assumption was that the PCs had a certain special something or they wouldn't be out adventuring instead of staying at home and being regular folks. Made sense that their stats would reflect this, so method (d) became our default, and was, in fact, the method used by the players in our current campaign.<br /> <br /> We might also note that the various point buy methods and such used over the years certainly are fine, too, but never had that bit of uncertainty that rolling the dice has, even with adding and subtracting points by various methods. I dunno, just never seemed to strike the right note for the CRC, maybe it was just too &quot;averaged out&quot; for me. I will say that even using method (d), we still add and subtract to/from the stats based on cultural and other reasons, so the dice rolling is still modified a tad.<br /> <br /> So, that's it, just a little bit on that magic moment when you see the numbers reveal or match up with what your character will be. It's a pretty unique thing to TTRPGs, really, and I do think it is part of the creative magic that draws players to our hobby!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5-richt","name":"RichT","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5_2.jpeg","title":"Onyx Path Creative Director","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"505"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}